// A tier block's own `use` may name a **package module**, not just a std one. The block-scope
// overlay qualifies `Thing` to `probe.side.Thing`, and the block's `use` now also drives *linking*,
// so the declaration that identity names is actually merged into the program. Before the fix
// nothing merged it: `noeta check` reported 0 errors (a qualified name it does not adjudicate) and
// `noeta test` failed every use site with "cannot find type `probe.side.Thing` in this scope",
// while the same shapes over std worked throughout — an extension module resolves through the
// registry and never needs the unit graph.
//
// This case pins the **inactive** half, which is all the harness can see (it never activates a
// tier): with no tier active the whole block is stripped before lowering, so the `use`, the fn, and
// every reference vanish together. The merged declarations are inert — each carries a *qualified*
// identity, so it binds no short name and nothing references it — so the program's own output is
// unchanged and both backends lower the identical stripped program. Nothing is left behind either:
// the import stays inside the block rather than being hoisted, so no dangling `use` binds `Thing`.
// (The active half — the block's `use` making `Thing` resolve under `noeta test` — is gated by the
// `noeta test` CLI tests.)
// expect: stdout "7"
// expect: stdout "done"
// expect: exit 0
namespace App.Main;

fn double(n: int): int {
    return n * 2
}

@test {
    use probe.side.{Thing}

    fn builds(): void {
        x = Thing { n: 3 }
        assert(x.n == 3)
    }
}

@bench {
    use probe.side

    fn whole_module(): void {
        assert(side.make() == 3)
    }
}

echo double(3) + 1
echo "done"
